{
  "pages": [
    {
      "page_idx": 3,
      "blocks": [
        {
          "type": "header",
          "bbox": [
            0.551,
            0.069,
            0.815,
            0.087
          ],
          "content": "Exploring Algebraic Identities"
        },
        {
          "type": "page_number",
          "bbox": [
            0.855,
            0.069,
            0.883,
            0.084
          ],
          "content": "71"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.108,
            0.885,
            0.146
          ],
          "content": "For example, $x ^ { 2 } - 1 = 2 4$ is true for only $x = 5  or  -5$ . Hence, it is an equation."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.111,
            0.146,
            0.883,
            0.186
          ],
          "content": "But (x $+ y ) ^ { 2 } = x ^ { 2 } + 2 x y + y ^ { 2 }$ is true for all values of x and y. Therefore, this equation is also an identity."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.191,
            0.884,
            0.228
          ],
          "content": "By now you must have observed that $( a + b ) ^ { 2 } \\neq a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 }$ . But can you find out which one of them will be greater?"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.113,
            0.229,
            0.885,
            0.267
          ],
          "content": "For example, if $a = 1 0$ and $b = 2$ , what are the values of $( a + b ) ^ { 2 }$ and $a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.272,
            0.746,
            0.292
          ],
          "content": "(a + b)2 = (10 + 2)2 = 122 = 144 and $a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 } = 1 0 ^ { 2 } + 2 ^ { 2 } = 1 0 4$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.298,
            0.485,
            0.317
          ],
          "content": "So in this case, $( a + b ) ^ { 2 } \\geq a ^ { 2 } + b ^ { 2 }$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.323,
            0.56,
            0.343
          ],
          "content": "But is it true for all numbers a and b?"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.362,
            0.398,
            0.385
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Think and Reflect"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.399,
            0.673,
            0.418
          ],
          "content": "1. What can you say about a and b if $(a + b)^{2} < a^{2} + b^{2} ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.159,
            0.423,
            0.672,
            0.441
          ],
          "content": "2. What can you say about a and b if $(a + b)^{2} \\geq a^{2} + b^{2}?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.158,
            0.446,
            0.542,
            0.464
          ],
          "content": "3. When will $( a + b ) ^ { 2 }$ be equal to $a^{2} + b^{2} ?$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.128,
            0.468,
            0.855,
            0.508
          ],
          "content": "Did you observe that $( a + b ) ^ { 2 }$ and $a^{2} + b^{2}$ are both positive? What term will decide which is larger? Use the expansion of $(a + b)^{2}$ to decide."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.523,
            0.886,
            0.563
          ],
          "content": "We can use the identity $(a + b)^{2} = a^{2} + 2ab + b^{2}$ to find the square of general binomial expressions."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.112,
            0.567,
            0.885,
            0.608
          ],
          "content": "**Example 3:** Let us try to expand $(5x + 2y)^{2}$ . Here $a ~ = ~ 5 x$ and $b = 2 y$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.614,
            0.782,
            0.635
          ],
          "content": "Then $(5x + 2y)^{2} = (5x)^{2} + 2(5x)(2y) + (2y)^{2} = 25x^{2} + 20xy + 4y^{2}.$"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.148,
            0.643,
            0.879,
            0.663
          ],
          "content": "We can also use the identity to help us with numerical calculations."
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.149,
            0.671,
            0.659,
            0.691
          ],
          "content": "**Example 4:** To calculate $4 3 ^ { 2 } ,$ we can write it as"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.15,
            0.698,
            0.727,
            0.718
          ],
          "content": "(40 + 3)^{2} = 40^{2} + 2 \\times 40 \\times 3 + 3^{2} = 1600 + 240 + 9 = 1849.",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_19.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "paragraph_title",
          "bbox": [
            0.375,
            0.755,
            0.621,
            0.782
          ],
          "level": 2,
          "content": "Exercise Set 4.1"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.132,
            0.803,
            0.849,
            0.825
          ],
          "content": "1. Using the identity $(a+b)^{2}=a^{2}+2ab+b^{2},$ , expand the following:"
        },
        {
          "type": "text",
          "bbox": [
            0.183,
            0.845,
            0.21,
            0.865
          ],
          "content": "(i)"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.23,
            0.845,
            0.439,
            0.866
          ],
          "content": "( 7 x + 4 y ) ^ { 2 }\\tag{ii}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_23.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.461,
            0.833,
            0.703,
            0.881
          ],
          "content": "\\left( { \\frac { 7 } { 5 } } x   +   { \\frac { 3 } { 2 } } y \\right) ^ { 2 }\\tag{iii}",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_24.jpg"
        },
        {
          "type": "equation",
          "bbox": [
            0.72,
            0.844,
            0.861,
            0.867
          ],
          "content": "( 2 . 5 p \\pm 1 . 5 q ) ^ { 2 }",
          "image_source": "images/page_3_equation_25.jpg"
        }
      ]
    }
